::Q1::What is a key difference between static analysis and AI assisted code analysis?
{
~AI assisted analysis always produces the same findings for the same code
=Static analysis uses fixed rules, while AI assisted analysis reasons over learned patterns and context
~Static analysis can understand developer intent better than AI assisted analysis
~AI assisted analysis guarantees that every reported finding is correct
}

::Q2::Why should a developer profile code before asking an AI tool to optimize it?
{
~Profiling automatically refactors the code before the AI tool sees it
~AI tools cannot analyze code unless a profiler has been used
~Profiling removes the need to run tests after an optimization
=It identifies where runtime is actually being spent, allowing optimization effort to target the real bottleneck
}

::Q3::What is the correct workflow for AI-assisted refactoring?
{
~Request the refactoring, update failing tests, then apply the change
~Apply several changes at once and measure the final result
=Confirm tests pass, request and review the refactoring, apply it, then run the tests again
~Replace the existing implementation first and write tests only if problems occur
}

::Q4::Which limitation applies to AI tools when analyzing code for performance?
{
~They cannot identify algorithmic complexity problems
=They do not know realistic data sizes and cannot measure which functions actually consume runtime
~They can only analyze code that contains syntax errors
~They always require access to the production environment
}

::Q5::What does a clean result from an AI-assisted security review mean?
{
~The code has passed a complete security audit
~The code contains no vulnerabilities
~The AI has verified that all security practices are correctly implemented
=Nothing was recognized by the tool; further security review is still necessary
}
